# install/windows/cursors.pin - the pointer Arch draws, on Windows: GNOME's Adwaita cursors in
# GNOME's own Windows build (adwaita-icon-theme, Adwaita/cursors);
# docs/design/iconography/README.md.
#
# <role> <url> <sha256> =<name>
#   fonts.pin's format; the id is the Windows pointer role the file fills (HKCU\Control
#   Panel\Cursors names it so). install/windows/apply-windows.sh downloads each URL once into
#   ~/.cache/dram/cursors, refuses it unless its sha256 is the one here, and stages it as <name>
#   in C:\ProgramData\dram\cursors beside a copy of this file and a NOTICE; the desktop
#   role's cursors-machine unit checks each against this file again and copies it to
#   %SystemRoot%\Cursors\Adwaita, and pointer-adwaita makes the user's "Adwaita" scheme of it.
#   A role not listed keeps Windows Black's cursor: Adwaita has no pen, up arrow, pin or person.
#
# adwaita-icon-theme 51.0 (2026-09-14): 31 .cur and 2 .ani files, each .cur five 32-bit images
# (96, 64, 48, 32, 24 px, a hotspot each), so Windows' smallest size (32, Arch draws 24) is
# drawn, not scaled. GNOME dropped the set from master on 2026-09-21 (5fad489e, to an
# adwaita-icon-theme-legacy), so this pin is where it lives on; Arch's Xcursor Adwaita goes
# the same way at 52 (packages/register.tsv). Licence: LGPL-3.0 or CC-BY-SA-3.0 US, credited
# as "GNOME Project" (COPYING at 51.0; the NOTICE says so). Bump: a tag that still ships
# Adwaita/cursors, each file's sha256 (shasum -a 256 on a download you trust), then
# apply-windows.sh.
